॰ `क्षेत्रसीमा` that fill it. No binding in this file is fed by a producer
॰ spelled differently from itself.
॰
॰ THE READING IS THE SAME ONE THE TABLES ALREADY GAVE, TWICE OVER, AND THAT IS
॰ WHY THIS UNIT NEEDED NO NEW ONE. `spec/fence-domains-riscv64.tsv`'s only
॰ numeric column is a BIT, read by `encode.rs:353`'s `f[2].parse::<u64>()` —
॰ an unsigned parse target the derived-table guard already pins — and the twin
॰ `domain_set` (`encode.rs:344`) returns `Option<u64>`, which is
॰ `ददाति सम्भाव्य न६४` here. Everything else in the four routines is a byte
॰ offset into that table's text or into the caller's own `मूल`, and
॰ `क्षेत्रखण्डसाम्यम्` IS `क्षेत्रसाम्यम्` with its `नाम` taken as a range
॰ instead of whole — the comment above each of the two says so — so the register
॰ table's reading transfers to it rather than being made a second time.
॰
॰ AND THE SENTINEL TEST THIS UNIT PASSES FOR THE SAME REASON THE REGISTER
॰ TABLE DID. `समूहांशः` answers ० for *no such domain* and `अवग्रहान्वेषणम्`
॰ answers `अवसानम्` for *no separator found* — a one-past-the-end offset, not
॰ a negative. Neither is -१, so neither earns a sign; the ० is sound only
॰ because every bit in the table is a SET bit, which is a fact about the data
॰ and is asserted where a test can see it rather than assumed here.
॰
॰ `the_fence_ordering_domain_table_is_unsigned_because_its_table_is` pins all
॰ of the above, and item 3 of
॰ `the_derived_table_text_reader_is_unsigned_because_its_tables_are` — which
॰ held `क्षेत्रखण्डसाम्यम्` at `अ६४` as this cycle's handle — is DELETED with
॰ this unit rather than edited, as that check's own comment instructed.
॰
॰ *The account of THE REGISTER TABLE follows and is kept, because the bound it
॰ stated is still the bound on the sites it did not take:*
॰
॰ THE PART CYCLE 526 TOOK WAS **THE REGISTER TABLE** — the four routines under
॰ `the register table`: क्षेत्रसाम्यम्, कोष्ठपङ्क्तिः, कोष्ठाङ्कः and कोष्ठप्लवः.
॰ 19 sites and the census sees all nineteen — the unit wraps no 64-bit type
॰ in `अङ्कः अन्तः`, `सम्भाव्य` or `दोषयुक्त`, so the file's count and the
॰ ledger move together. `encode.t1` carried 134 `अ६४` and 52 `न६४`; it
॰ carries 115 and 71 now. Corpus ledger 669/0/709 -> **650/0/728**, sum
॰ still 1378. THE FILE IS STILL NOT REMEDIATED: the fence table below is
॰ untouched, as are the head declarations, the slot-kind predicates, the
॰ encoding accessors, bytes-and-words and diagnostics.
॰
॰ THIS IS THE FIRST UNIT OF THE REPAIR THAT CLOSES A DISAGREEMENT INSTEAD
॰ OF OPENING ONE. The derived-table text reader (below) moved the PRODUCERS
॰ and deliberately left fifteen bindings in the two table sections declaring
॰ `अ६४` while filled from an `न६४` return; TEN of those fifteen are in this
॰ unit — the `:1200`, `:1201`, `:1234`, `:1264`-`:1266` and `:1287`-`:1289`
॰ of that note — and they now agree with what fills them. The FIVE left are
॰ the fence table's and are the next cycle's.
॰
॰ AND THE DISAGREEMENT CROSSED A MODULE BOUNDARY, WHICH IS THE PART NO
॰ CENSUS COULD HAVE FOUND. `samyojana.t1`'s `भेदपङ्क्तिः` — itself
॰ remediated — walks its own table with the same nine producers and called
॰ `सङ्केतन ॱ क्षेत्रसाम्यम्` with `न६४` arguments into `अ६४` parameters.
॰ A call site is not a type position, so neither end of it is counted.
॰
॰ THE READING IS ONE READING AND NOT NINETEEN, and its twin is
॰ `encode.rs:314`'s `pub fn register(name: &str) -> Option<(u32, bool)>`.
॰ That return settles the only site the tables do not: the register NUMBER,
॰ `f[2].parse()` landing in that `u32`. Everything else is a byte offset
॰ into `spec/registers-riscv64.tsv`'s text, a TAB-separated field index, or
॰ a loop counter over a name's octets — offsets that exist here only because
॰ Rust walks the table with `.lines()` and T1 has no iterator.
॰
॰ THE ONE-BASED ANSWERS ARE WHY THIS UNIT COULD HAVE EARNED A SIGN AND DID
॰ NOT. `कोष्ठपङ्क्तिः` answers ० for *no such register* and `कोष्ठाङ्कः` adds
॰ १ to the parsed number, because `शून्यः` IS register ० and a raw number
॰ would report the most-used register in the ISA as an unknown name. -१ is
॰ the shape that would have made these signed; the port did not reach for it.
॰ `the_register_table_is_unsigned_because_a_register_number_is` pins all of
॰ the above.
॰
॰ *The account of THE DERIVED-TABLE TEXT READER follows and is kept, because
॰ the bound it stated is still the bound on the sites it did not take:*
॰
॰ THE PART CYCLE 525 TOOK WAS **THE DERIVED-TABLE TEXT READER** — the nine routines
॰ under `reading a derived table's TEXT`, 38 sites, of which 36 the census can
॰ see. `encode.t1` carried 170 `अ६४` and 16 `न६४`; it carries 134 and 52 now.
॰ Corpus ledger 705/0/673 -> **669/0/709**, sum still 1378.
॰
॰ THE NINE ROUTINES: अष्टकान्वेषणम्, पङ्क्तिसीमा, उपेक्ष्यपङ्क्तिः,
॰ क्षेत्रसंख्या, क्षेत्रारम्भः, क्षेत्रसीमा, षोडशाङ्कः, षोडशाङ्कमूल्यम् and
॰ दशाङ्कमूल्यम्. THEY NEEDED NO PER-SITE READING AND THAT IS THE POINT OF
॰ TAKING THEM AS ONE: every site in them is a byte OFFSET into a table's text,
॰ a COUNT of fields, or a value PARSED out of a field, and the twin makes all
॰ three unsigned by construction. The offsets are Rust `usize` (`lines()`,
॰ `split('\t')`, `split_once` — encode.rs:265-296 and :344-362); the counts are
॰ `f.len()`; and every `parse()` in the four readers lands in an unsigned type
॰ and no other — `bias: u32` (`encode.rs:67`, read at `:287`), `pattern`/`mask`
॰ `u32::from_str_radix` (`:295`, `:296`), a register number, and the fence
॰ domain bit `f[2].parse::<u64>()` (`:353`). The four `.tsv` tables hold no
॰ signed column at all.
॰
॰ WHY THE PRODUCERS AND NOT A CONSUMER: these nine are what the register table
॰ (`कोष्ठाङ्कः`, `कोष्ठपङ्क्तिः`, `क्षेत्रसाम्यम्`) and the fence table
॰ (`क्षेत्रसमूहः`, `समूहांशः`, `क्षेत्रखण्डसाम्यम्`, `अवग्रहान्वेषणम्`) are
॰ BUILT OUT OF, and cycle 524's own rule — *an index whose producer is outside
॰ the unit keeps its producer's spelling until the producer moves* — makes the
॰ producer the only end that can move first. Taking a table reader first would
॰ have spelled `न६४` on bindings fed by `अ६४` returns for no gain.
॰
॰ THE BOUND, STATED SO THE NEXT CYCLE KNOWS WHAT IT INHERITS: the nine
॰ routines moved and NOTHING THAT CALLS THEM DID. Fifteen bindings in the two
॰ table sections below — `:1200`, `:1201`, `:1234`, `:1264`-`:1266`, `:1287`-
॰ `:1289`, `:1368`, `:1369`, `:1412` and `:1417`-`:1419` — now declare `अ६४`
॰ and are filled from a `न६४` return. THAT DISAGREEMENT IS
॰ DELIBERATE AND IS THE HANDLE FOR THE NEXT UNIT: it is exactly the set the
॰ register-table cycle and the fence-table cycle have to move, and it is
॰ visible at the call site rather than needing to be searched for. The two
॰ sections are otherwise untouched, as are the head declarations, the slot-kind
॰ predicates, the encoding accessors, bytes-and-words, diagnostics and
॰ everything the signed half already took.
॰
॰ *Cycle 524's account of THE SIGNED HALF follows and is kept, because the
॰ bound it stated is still the bound on the sites it did not take:*
॰
॰ THE PART CYCLE 524 TOOK WAS **THE SIGNED HALF** — the eleven routines that
॰ held every one of the file's 18 `इ६४`, respelled together under ADR-0031.
॰ `encode.t1` carried 171 `अ६४` and 15 census `इ६४` (18 counting the three that
॰ sit two words past `ॱॱ` as a slice ELEMENT and so are invisible to the
॰ census); it carried 170 `अ६४`, 0 `इ६४` and 16 `न६४` after. Corpus ledger
॰ 706/15/657 -> 705/0/673, sum still 1378.
॰
॰ **THE `इ६४` COLUMN OF ADR-0030 IS ZERO AND THE SIGNED HALF OF THE REPAIR
॰ IS FINISHED.** Every `इ६४` left anywhere under `crates/sadhana-t1/src/` is
॰ inside a `॰` comment describing this repair. What remains of ADR-0030 is the
॰ unsigned direction alone: 134 `अ६४` here and 487 in `vakyavibhaga.t1`.
॰
॰ THE ELEVEN ROUTINES, AND WHY THEY ARE ONE UNIT RATHER THAN A LINE RANGE:
॰ स्थापनम्, अवकाशविस्तारः, अन्तर्भावः, चिह्नितान्तर्भावः (the field-placement
॰ section), स्थानाधारः, उपरिखण्डम्, स्थानमूल्यम् (the two halves of an address
॰ and the value one carries), विन्यासविचलनम्, पदरचना, अपाकर्तव्यम् and
॰ सङ्कोचनिषेधः. A signed site and its unsigned partner are declared on the SAME
॰ line, so the signed half cannot be taken by line range — taking it by ROUTINE
॰ is what keeps a pair from being half-respelled, which is the one state that
॰ makes a pair invisible: `मूल्यम् ॱॱ अ६४ चिह्नितम् ॱॱ अ६४` reads as a
॰ duplicated argument and not as two readings of one number.
॰
॰ THE BOUND, STATED SO THE NEXT CYCLE KNOWS WHAT IT INHERITS: inside those
॰ eleven, the NUMERIC quantities move — indices, counts, widths, bit patterns
॰ and the signed values themselves. The ENUM DISCRIMINANTS do not: `भेदाः` and
॰ `लिखितभेदः` in पदरचना, `भेदाः` and `सम्बन्धः` in अपाकर्तव्यम् are slot KINDS
॰ and a relation in सङ्केतन's own numbering, and their siblings — समानभेदः,
॰ कोष्ठवाचकः, अवकाश ॱ भेद — all sit OUTSIDE this unit, so they move with the
॰ enum and not with the signed half. Same for सङ्कोचनिषेधः's `गन्तृक्रमः`,
॰ which is bound straight from आवरणावकाशः's `अ६४` return: an index whose
॰ producer is outside the unit keeps its producer's spelling until the producer
॰ moves. Respelling either kind here would have put this file's own callers in
॰ disagreement to no purpose, which is the cost a bounded part is bounded to
॰ avoid.
॰
॰ AND ONE OF THE FOUR ROUTINES `.loop/STATE.md` PRE-JUDGED AS A DIFFERENCE IS
॰ NOT A DIFFERENCE. *Next task* named स्थानाधारः, उपरिखण्डम्, स्थानमूल्यम् and
॰ विन्यासविचलनम् as *"signed on their own merit"* because *a signed quantity in
॰ this port is almost always a DIFFERENCE*. Three of them are. स्थानाधारः is
॰ NOT: its parameter is Rust's `pc` and its result is Rust's `base`, and
॰ `encode.rs:533` declares `let mut pc = 0u32` while `:1490` writes `let base =
॰ if upper { pc } else { pc.wrapping_sub(4) }` — u32 on both sides. The site
॰ stays signed anyway, and the REASON is recorded at the routine rather than
॰ left as the one it was given, because a right spelling held for a wrong
॰ reason is what the next reader inherits.
॰ ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

