॰ ══════ ADDED 2026-08-31, AND THIS IMPORT IS THE ANSWER TO THE ADR ══════
॰
॰ THE QUESTION, as (c) below put it: `विश्लेषण ॱ पङ्क्तिसङ्केतः`
॰ (vishlesana.t1:815) already turns a row of spec/encodings-riscv64.tsv
॰ into a `सङ्केत`, slots and all — but `विश्लेषण` imports `सङ्केतन`, so
॰ calling it from here is a CYCLE, and the alternative is a second
॰ transcription of the one thing this file has refused to transcribe
॰ twice. It was recorded as "an ADR and not a coding decision, which is
॰ why the eight are still stubs and not half-written".
॰
॰ THE ANSWER IS THAT IT NEEDS NO ADR, AND THE REASON IS THAT THIS MODULE
॰ IS ALREADY IN TWO SUCH CYCLES — both of them deliberate, both of them
॰ green, and one of them added by the very session that then called a
॰ third one an ADR:
॰
॰   * `अक्षरकोश` imports `सङ्केतन` (sanskrit_text.t1:7) for the five-part
॰     TSV row walk, and `सङ्केतन` imports `अक्षरकोश` (line 12 above) for
॰     the numeral readers. Added 2026-08-30, the same day.
॰   * `निदान` imports `सङ्केतन` (nidana.t1:99) for the same row walk, and
॰     `सङ्केतन` imports `निदान` (line 17 above) for `विवरणम्`.
॰
॰ SO THE CYCLE IS NOT A NEW KIND OF THING and there is nothing for an ADR
॰ to rule on. It is also not merely tolerated by accident: `मण्डलम्`
॰ (crates/sadhana/src/t1/mandala.rs) models imports as a FLAT SET and
॰ checks exactly two properties — that a reached module is imported
॰ (`missing_imports`) and that an imported module exists
॰ (`UnknownImport`). There is no ordering, no acyclicity test and no link
॰ order to violate. `निर्वहण`'s loader agrees: `Interpreter::load`
॰ (nirvahana.rs:429) reads every source's head into ONE flat arity table
॰ keyed by both the bare and the qualified name, so a call resolves by
॰ name at call time and direction never enters into it.
॰
॰ VERIFIED, NOT REASONED: `the_encoder_reaches_the_row_reader_it_imports`
॰ in tests/t1_exec_encode.rs loads this file together with `vishlesana.t1`
॰ and asks `विश्लेषण ॱ पङ्क्तिसङ्केतः` for a row, and asserts the `सङ्केत`
॰ it answers against spec/encodings-riscv64.tsv read by the test itself.
॰ It was a probe before it was a test, and the probe is what settled this.
॰
॰ WHAT THE DECISION BUYS: the second transcription does not happen. This
॰ file has refused to hand-copy the encoding table twice, on the ground
॰ that "a hand-typed second copy makes the two agree by construction and
॰ checks nothing"; a hand-typed second ROW READER is the same defect one
॰ level down, and `पङ्क्तिसङ्केतः` is 60 lines of slot parsing that would
॰ have had to be duplicated exactly.
॰
॰ THIS IMPORT IS NOW SPENT — 2026-08-31. The note that stood here said it
॰ was "DECLARED AND NOT YET SPENT", and that the first routine to spend it
॰ would owe one row in `NEEDS_A_MODULE_NOT_LOADED`
॰ (tests/t1_execution.rs), which loads `encode.t1` and `vakyavibhaga.t1`
॰ and nothing else. Two routines spend it — `कुलक्षेत्रयोग्यम्`, which
॰ reads field ९ through `विश्लेषण ॱ क्षेत्रवाचकः`, and `कुलसङ्केतः`, which
॰ turns a candidate row into a `सङ्केत` through `पङ्क्तिसङ्केतः` — and the
॰ row was paid in the same commit. The warning was exactly right about
॰ what would be needed, which is the whole value of having written it
॰ down: the first use did not fail a test nobody expected.
आयातः विश्लेषण ।
आयातः वास्तु ।   ॰ the object record this file now BUILDS, not merely encodes for
आयातः पदविभाग ।   ॰ the spec-table store: `समावेशपाठः` answers a table by name (2026-09-14)
आयातः अष्टक ।   ॰ the OUTPUT CHANNEL, for `विन्यासमुद्रणम्` — zero imports, nothing cycles

