॰ the class letter a type name OPENS WITH — Rust's
॰ `inst.types[0].chars().next()` under `matches!(class, 'अ' | 'न' | 'प')`.
॰ १ is अ, २ is न, ३ is भ, and ० is EVERY OTHER OPENING, which is the whole
॰ of what the `matches!` adds.
॰
॰ `वाक्यविभाग ॱ प्रकारवर्णः` CANNOT STAND IN FOR THIS and the difference is
॰ not stylistic: it answers १ for a name with NO class letter, because
॰ there `ॱ३२` means a signed 32-bit value. That is a different question
॰ from whether a letter was written, and a port that asked it would refuse
॰ `योगःॱ३२` as contradicting a signed family.
॰ Each letter is one akṣara — २२४ १६४ and a third octet. A Devanagari
॰ DIGIT is २२४ १६५, which is why the second octet is tested at all.
॰ THE PARAMETER IS `प्रकारनाम` AND NOT `प्रकारः`: that word is in the FROZEN
॰ `keyword` production of spec/grammar-t1.ebnf, which says a keyword "is also
॰ a possible identifier — the reader must prefer the keyword", so binding it
॰ as a name is already illegal and is unnoticed only because no T1 front end
॰ enforces the list. `no_t1_source_binds_a_frozen_keyword_as_a_name` is the
॰ guard that caught it, exactly as `B-112` intended.
